    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Why Is Nutrition Important?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret spoken advice on common problems. Interactive: Engage in role-play exercises.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret spoken advice about nutrition and summarize key points about eating balanced meals and local Panamanian foods (e.g., "Summarize advice on eating balanced meals, such as including fresh tropical fruits like mangos or plantains."). Can engage in role-play exercises on healthy eating by offering suggestions (e.g., "You should try adding an apple to your lunch. It's a great source of vitamins and fiber!") Can engage in discussions by asking questions about food choices (e.g., "Why is it important to eat vegetables every day? What do they do for our bodies?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of pictures on the board or projector, showing various Panamanian foods (e.g., sancocho, fresh tropical fruits like mangoes, plantains, yuca, vs. processed snacks like chips and sodas). Students work in pairs to categorize them into "Healthy" or "Unhealthy" food groups. The teacher asks, "Which foods are healthy? Which foods are unhealthy? Why?" to activate prior knowledge related to nutrition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words related to nutrition for the listening activity. Teacher writes the words on the board: <i>nutrition, healthy, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, vitamins, minerals, protein, fiber, metabolism</i>. The teacher models the pronunciation, focusing on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "nu-TRI-tion," "me-TA-bol-ism," "VI-ta-mins"). The teacher provides simple definitions and example sentences for each word, e.g., "<i>Nutrition</i> is about eating good food for your body." or "A <i>balanced diet</i> includes many types of food."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary by asking Concept Checking Questions.
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'nutrition' about eating junk food or healthy food?" (Healthy food).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you eat a 'balanced diet,' do you eat only one type of food or many types?" (Many types).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are 'vitamins' good or bad for your health?" (Good).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will listen to a short audio recording (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) of a public service announcement or a dialogue between a teenager ("Carlos") and a nutritionist ("Dr. Elena") discussing common dietary problems (e.g., feeling tired, low energy) and offering advice on improving nutrition. The audio will incorporate the target vocabulary (<i>nutrition, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, protein, fiber, vitamins, minerals</i>) and grammatical structures (modal "should," first conditional, adverbial clauses for justification like "because it...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Interpret spoken advice on common problems and understand the benefits and justifications for healthy eating. The focus is on understanding the main points and specific details related to improving diet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen for the main idea. Teacher asks: "What is Carlos's problem?" and "What is Dr. Elena talking about in general?" (Suggested answers: Carlos is tired/lacks energy. Dr. Elena is giving advice on healthy eating/nutrition.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False & Matching):</b> Students listen again to identify specific advice and facts.
                        <ol style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Students mark statements as True or False based on the audio (e.g., "Dr. Elena says Carlos should eat more processed foods." - False; "Eating fruits and vegetables gives you vitamins." - True).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matching: Students match problems Carlos has with the solutions Dr. Elena suggests (e.g., "Feeling tired" matches "Eat a balanced breakfast").</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for and identifying the modal "should" for giving advice, the first conditional for facts related to nutrition, and adverbial clauses for justification, along with the target vocabulary (<i>balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, vitamins, protein, fiber</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Should/Shouldn't" Gap-fill:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the audio, missing "should" or "shouldn't." Students listen to specific segments of the audio again and fill in the gaps. Example: "You _____ eat more fruits and vegetables." "You _____ avoid too much sugar." (Answer key provided for self-correction.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – First Conditional Completion:</b> Students listen to the audio a third time, specifically for statements using the first conditional. The teacher provides sentence beginnings on a worksheet, and students complete the sentences based on what they hear. Example: "If you eat a balanced breakfast, you _____" (will have more energy). "If we eat a mix of foods, it _____" (will give us energy and help us stay healthy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Justification Check:</b> In pairs, students receive a list of advice statements from the audio (e.g., "You should eat more fruits."). They discuss and verbally recall the justification from the audio using "because" (e.g., "You should eat more fruits because they give you vitamins and fiber."). This activity encourages listening recall and basic spoken production, reinforcing adverbial clauses. The teacher circulates, monitoring for correct use of grammar and pronunciation of new words like "fiber" and "vitamins."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will engage in a role-play activity to practice offering dietary advice, directly connecting to the learning outcome of "Engage in role-play exercises on healthy eating by offering suggestions."</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play dialogues in pairs. Each pair will choose one scenario card (e.g., "A friend often feels tired in class," "A friend only eats processed snacks," "A friend wants to improve their digestion").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ol style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form pairs. One student is "the friend with a problem," and the other is "the nutrition advisor."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "advisor" offers advice using "You should/shouldn't..." and justifies it using "because..." or explains benefits with "If you..., you will..." The advisor must incorporate at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, vitamins, protein, fiber</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "friend with a problem" listens, asks questions for clarification (e.g., "Why is it important to eat vegetables every day? What do they do for our bodies?"), and responds to the advice.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs practice their dialogues. The teacher provides a few minutes for preparation and encourages the use of local Panamanian food examples (e.g., "You should try adding fresh tropical fruits like mangoes or plantains to your breakfast for more vitamins.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Selected pairs perform their role-plays for the class.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in offering advice and asking questions, collaborative interaction, and correct application of modal "should," first conditional, adverbial clauses, and target vocabulary in a realistic nutritional advice context.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the role-play performances. The checklist focuses on the accurate use of "should" and "shouldn't," the first conditional, appropriate vocabulary from the theme, and clear pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their role-play dialogues on giving and receiving nutritional advice. The teacher observes their ability to interpret and offer advice effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Pronounces target vocabulary (e.g., "nutrition," "metabolism," "vitamins") clearly, with appropriate stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 3-4 new vocabulary words (e.g., *balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, vitamins, fiber*) in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Advice is clear, logical, and easy to understand. Uses "should" and first conditional correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively listens and responds appropriately in the role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new piece of advice about nutrition that I learned today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Could I confidently give advice to a friend about eating healthy using 'should' and giving reasons?" (Yes/Partially/No)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did listening to the nutritionist's advice help me understand 'Why is nutrition important'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson (new words, grammar, giving advice) was most challenging for me, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The listening comprehension activities were generally successful, especially the matching task. Students showed good understanding of the main ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with differentiating specific minimal pairs during pronunciation practice, and consistent application of stress in longer words like "carbohydrates" will need more attention.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The role-play was engaging, but a few students needed more prompting to use adverbial clauses for justification ("because it..."). More sentence frames might be needed in future lessons for this structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For the next lesson, I will ensure the reading text clearly reinforces the same vocabulary and grammatical structures, offering another input mode for reinforcing these concepts.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short article or blog post about the benefits of a balanced diet and specific nutrients, building on the vocabulary and concepts introduced through listening in this lesson. They will encounter the same terms and advice in a written format to deepen their understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>